  "style": {
    "name": "Verdigris Sun Temple (Mesoamerican stepped terrace in copper, gold and terracotta)",
    "rationale": "A Maya/Aztec-inspired stepped temple front that narrows in four tiers (31, 23, 13 and 9 blocks wide). The silhouette is a stepped pyramid with a crowning roof-comb. A red terracotta base tier carries oxidized-copper pilasters on an even 4-block bay rhythm, with gold capitals and polished blackstone plinths. Between the pilasters sit four framed windows. Each has a gilded blackstone surround, a dark prismarine inset and a sea-lantern glow. A yellow glazed terracotta frieze runs above the windows. The grand central portal is deep: its two-block-deep recess is cut by exclusion, with a corbelled Maya-vault arch in cut copper stairs, blackstone piers and a gold lintel. The back wall is dark prismarine lit by sea lanterns. A broad blackstone stair climbs to it, flanked by soul-lantern posts. The orange second tier holds a gold-and-lantern sun medallion over the door and prismarine lamp panels. The cyan third tier is banded with purple glazed terracotta. Gold merlon teeth sit on the shoulders and the crest is topped with a sea lantern. Projecting cornices at y11, y17 and y21 use inverted stairs for shadowed mouldings. Relief comes from pilasters at z3-4, cornices, window frames and a recessed doorway. The palette contrasts warm (red, orange, gold) against cool (verdigris, cyan, prismarine) over black stone, deliberately avoiding pale quartz or sandstone."
  },
